.TH tapectl-drive-cleaned 1  "tapectl-drive-cleaned " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-drive\-cleaned \- Record that the drive was cleaned — a record, not an action
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl drive cleaned\fR [\fB\-\-cartridge\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Record that the drive was cleaned — a record, not an action
.PP
tapectl drives no changer and never loads a cartridge, so cleaning is you inserting a cleaning cartridge when the drive asks for one (`drive status` shows "asks to be CLEANED"; `drive poll` reports the cleaning TapeAlert flag). This command writes down that it happened: one `cleaned` event on the drive, and, when a registered cleaning cartridge did it, one use of that cartridge. `report health` shows each drive\*(Aqs last cleaning and each cleaning cartridge\*(Aqs recorded uses against its rated life.
.PP
Which cleaning cartridge: `\-\-cartridge SERIAL`, or, without it, the one in the drive if its chip names a registered cleaning cartridge (read through the SCSI generic node; nothing moves, and nothing is sent while a tapectl command holds the drive). With neither, the cleaning is recorded against the drive alone and no cartridge\*(Aqs use is counted. Register a cleaning cartridge first with `drive cleaning\-cartridge add`.
.PP
Exit 0: recorded. Exit 1: recorded, and the cleaning cartridge is at or past its rated life (a spent cartridge does not clean; replace it). A cartridge near its rated life is a warning with exit 0. Exit 2: not recorded — the drive gave no serial to record against, or the named cartridge is not registered.
.PP
The chip read that identifies the cartridge in the drive is kept whole in the MAM journal (`cartridge journal`), against no contact.
.PP
`\-\-dry\-run` reports what would be recorded and records nothing. With `\-\-json` the whole of stdout is one JSON object.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-cartridge\fR \fI<CARTRIDGE>\fR
The cleaning cartridge used, by the serial it was registered under (`drive cleaning\-cartridge add`)
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The drive that was cleaned, by its tape node path as configured (`[[backends.lto]].device_tape`; a by\-id link). Defaults to the only configured drive
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
